      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763835951841,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A new learning paradigm: Reinforcement Learning",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A new learning paradigm: Reinforcement Learning",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hfBK_0YI21L3VegsA4iG2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2114.8286635784016,
      "y": 3358.4530640495827,
      "width": 904.2855631510411,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "xLc7yDfFDzDviSpafFyK3",
        "dt5UlJkTJ-RISGLP2Ik63"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1505218818,
      "version": 1652,
      "versionNonce": 527529678,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763835951841,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next-token prediction --> imitation learning\nReinforcement learning --> learning from interaction\n\nLearning from interaction (trial-and-error) is a foundational idea underlying nearly all\ntheories of learning and intelligence. Exercising this connection produces a wealth of\ninformation about cause and effect, about the consequences of actions, and about what to\ndo in order to achieve goals. Reinforcement learning explores a computational approach to\nlearning from interaction. \n\nReinforcement learning problems involve learning what to do so as to maximize a numerical\nreward signal.  The learner is not told which actions to take, as in supervised machine\nlearning, but instead must discover which actions yield the most reward by trying them out.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next-token prediction --> imitation learning\nReinforcement learning --> learning from interaction\n\nLearning from interaction (trial-and-error) is a foundational idea underlying nearly all theories of learning and intelligence. Exercising this connection produces a wealth of information about cause and effect, about the consequences of actions, and about what to do in order to achieve goals. Reinforcement learning explores a computational approach to learning from interaction. \n\nReinforcement learning problems involve learning what to do so as to maximize a numerical reward signal.  The learner is not told which actions to take, as in supervised machine learning, but instead must discover which actions yield the most reward by trying them out.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
