      "updated": 1763995415203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Should you bother with language models?",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Should you bother with language models?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CnpEDbD1CSe19NHhFTq1W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1514.536928159382,
      "y": -2329.8763184356258,
      "width": 761.539306640625,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "FKns1Lq5x5e2B1ZFu_rD_"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 216483359,
      "version": 2109,
      "versionNonce": 1865020293,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763995420325,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Academic exercise: understand the mechanisms underlying language models \n    and gain the ability to interpret their outputs responsibly.\n2. Professional responsability: explain these nuances to non-experts who rely \n    on these tools and guide business leaders making strategic decisions\n3. Social responsability: AI becomes increasingly embedded in our lifes, the \n    field needs experts can bridge the gap between complex mathematics and \n    human understanding. Your knowledge becomes a safeguard against \n    potential harms or misinterpretation of AI \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Academic exercise: understand the mechanisms underlying language models \n    and gain the ability to interpret their outputs responsibly.\n2. Professional responsability: explain these nuances to non-experts who rely \n    on these tools and guide business leaders making strategic decisions\n3. Social responsability: AI becomes increasingly embedded in our lifes, the \n    field needs experts can bridge the gap between complex mathematics and \n    human understanding. Your knowledge becomes a safeguard against \n    potential harms or misinterpretation of AI \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FkmTvMHLZL_D7Wy6QPubb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -570.0937712819336,
      "y": -3099.973881040864,
      "width": 327.0162048339845,
      "height": 232.95149301123604,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1367173855,
      "version": 1277,
      "versionNonce": 1206198667,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "gpdjlsn9i1UIvWPj3ePlD",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1763995440365,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do we estimate these n-gram probabilities? \nMaximum likelihood estimation: use  observed data as\nbest guess for the future.\n\n\n\n\nWhat happens when a new combination of n words\nappears that was not seen in the training corpus?\nWe must make a Markovian assumption\n\n\n\n\nLanguage is still too creative/sparse for n-grams...",
      "fontSize": 12.424079627265922,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we estimate these n-gram probabilities? \nMaximum likelihood estimation: use  observed data as best guess for the future.\n\n\n\n\nWhat happens when a new combination of n words appears that was not seen in the training corpus?\nWe must make a Markovian assumption\n\n\n\n\nLanguage is still too creative/sparse for n-grams...",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "A4xw0cBqe6z4kav-ZJG7K",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -169.3393720504314,
      "y": -3093.255771226315,
      "width": 338.5162048339845,
      "height": 217.42139347715363,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1609440639,
      "version": 1262,
      "versionNonce": 347036651,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763995448546,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A neural probabilistic language model in two parts:\n    1. A mapping C from any element of V to a real \n        vector C(i)\n    2. a function g(·) maps concatenated feature\nvectors [C(t-n), ··· , C(t-1)] to a probability distribution\nover words in V\n\nThe function g may be \nimplemented by a \nfeed-forward or any another\nparameterized function.\nNow the LM learns the word \nfeature vectors and the \nparameters of g",
      "fontSize": 12.424079627265922,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A neural probabilistic language model in two parts:\n    1. A mapping C from any element of V to a real \n        vector C(i)\n    2. a function g(·) maps concatenated feature\nvectors [C(t-n), ··· , C(t-1)] to a probability distribution over words in V\n\nThe function g may be \nimplemented by a \nfeed-forward or any another\nparameterized function.\nNow the LM learns the word \nfeature vectors and the \nparameters of g",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "N_EeHq_tlevzfIYkGTQHy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1086.94842753768,
      "y": -1641.8279864419546,
      "width": 730.993408203125,
      "height": 460,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8R",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2013242192,
      "version": 1076,
      "versionNonce": 1030987051,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763995455120,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An untrained model generates random vector at the beginning.\n\nModel objective: increase the probability in the index  positions corresponding to the \ncorrect target token IDs, the higher the probability in the correct positions, the better.\n    --> the loss function measures how far off the model's predictions are \n        from the target values.\n\nThe model weights are optimized to decrease the loss --> to receive less penalization\n\nThe update is done via an algorithm called backpropagation (next lecture)\n\nIn language modeling (and in general all classification tasks) the loss of choice is the \ncross entropy loss: \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis loss measures the difference between two probability distributions; the one generated\nby the language model head and the real distribution. The true distribution of tokens is\nrepresented by a one-hot vector, where the position of the 1 correspondes to the vocabulary\nID of the token to be predicted.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An untrained model generates random vector at the beginning.\n\nModel objective: increase the probability in the index  positions corresponding to the \ncorrect target token IDs, the higher the probability in the correct positions, the better.\n    --> the loss function measures how far off the model's predictions are \n        from the target values.\n\nThe model weights are optimized to decrease the loss --> to receive less penalization\n\nThe update is done via an algorithm called backpropagation (next lecture)\n\nIn language modeling (and in general all classification tasks) the loss of choice is the \ncross entropy loss: \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis loss measures the difference between two probability distributions; the one generated\nby the language model head and the real distribution. The true distribution of tokens is\nrepresented by a one-hot vector, where the position of the 1 correspondes to the vocabulary\nID of the token to be predicted.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SliTaglF_ezPC_QTkvOuA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1801.483326696136,
      "y": 600.7374852318708,
      "width": 773.0797882080071,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8S",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 643782576,
      "version": 1259,
      "versionNonce": 974612133,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763995471413,
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155#page=37",
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Goal: instill a communicative intent in the stochastoc parrot\nThis intent has three pillars:\n \n1. helpful (the agent should help the user solve their task)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2. harmless (the agent should not cause physical, psychological, or social harm\nto people or the environment)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. honest (the agent shouldn’t fabricate information or mislead the user)\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Goal: instill a communicative intent in the stochastoc parrot\nThis intent has three pillars:\n \n1. helpful (the agent should help the user solve their task)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2. harmless (the agent should not cause physical, psychological, or social harm to people or the environment)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. honest (the agent shouldn’t fabricate information or mislead the user)\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
